// The tokenizer. Every scanner takes (src, i) and returns the index just past
// what it consumed; the top loop does nothing but thread that index. The
// per-character decision is a chain of two-way branches writing through a
// one-element `out` cell, because a three-way `if/else if/else` plus the
// declaration it needs would be four actions -- and because the fall-through arm
// must not be evaluated when an earlier arm matched, which rules out the
// "call the tail first, then override" trick used for pure functions.

// The loop asks charat rather than len, which is the difference between a linear
// scan and a quadratic one. `len(s)` is a strlen every time it is evaluated, so
// `while (i < len(src))` re-measured the whole source once per character -- 130 KB
// of game gets read 130 000 times. charat remembers the length of the last string
// it saw, so asking it for a byte past the end (-1) is O(1) and is also the
// termination test. One string, one measurement, one pass.
lex_run(string src) {
  int i = 0;
  while (charat(src, i) >= 0) {
    i = lex_one(src, i);
  }
  lex_run_eof();
} return void;

lex_one(string src, int i) {
  int[] out = [0];
  lex_one1(src, i, out);
  int ret_i = out[0];
} return int ret_i;
